<script setup lang="ts">
import { computed,ref } from 'vue';
defineProps<{mode:string}>();
const query=ref(''),selected=ref('setup');
const topics=[
 {id:'setup',title:'First-time setup',text:'Open Data and State → Importer and choose your original Scout progress workbook. Review the detected Scouts, completed cells and unmatched rows, then apply the import. Choose Scouts from the roster in Scout scope. Use Planner to browse requirements, build a schedule and manage activities. Download a full backup from Exporter when setup is complete.'},
 {id:'workbook',title:'Importing the original workbook',text:'Choose an XLSX, XLS or XLSM workbook in Importer. Sheet names must identify the stage and Camping, Bushwalking or Bushcraft stream. A parseable date or a non-default filled assessment cell counts as completion. Blank cells do not erase earlier evidence. Unmatched requirement rows are preserved as workbook-specific requirements. Always review the preview and verify results: workbook styles can vary.'},
 {id:'scope',title:'Choosing Scouts',text:'Scout scope lists the Scouts found in your imported progress. Choose All imported Scouts, one Scout, or Choose a subset to tick several Scouts. The chosen scope updates Trailmap readiness and the Requirements Tracker. If the list is empty, import a progress workbook or Scout-progress dataset first. No names need to be typed.'},
 {id:'trailmap',title:'Reading the Trailmap and recording progress',text:'Stages run left to right; each stage groups Camping, Bushwalking and Bushcraft in that order. Use the separate filter panel to search by text or narrow by stage, stream, status or theme. ✓ means complete, → means ready, and ! means prerequisites are missing. A group is complete only when every selected Scout has completed that requirement. Select a requirement to view its evidence or record individual completion. Stage 1 has no readiness blockers. Changing workspaces closes the requirement editor.'},
 {id:'schedule',title:'Generating and changing a schedule',text:'Open Planner → Scheduler. Choose a strategy, week count (10 by default), capacity, themes to prioritise and allowed locations. Selected themes rotate as tie preferences; the planner compares their whole-night coverage. Scouts are grouped by projected stage first, then permitted venues and alternative statement sets are compared across all active groups. Any Scouts still without work are listed by name and reason. Planning shows weekly progress and can be cancelled. Set a target per stage per night. Related themes on planned skills can fill shortages; those additions are marked Suggested. Location-incompatible and unrelated skills are left out, with shortages shown. Theme/tag matches are the primary decision; readiness and strategy break ties. Homework is available for log-book tasks. Generate schedule shows a preview before saving. Regenerate unlocked preserves locked weeks. Each week has a theme selector and progression or random selection method. Preview themed week reviews the replacement requirements; applying locks that week and clears its old activity attachment and session confirmations. Other weeks and Scout evidence stay unchanged. Use the opposing-arrow Swap control to exchange two unlocked week plans. The padlock button locks or unlocks a week. A locked week can be deliberately replaced through this explicit theme preview. Click a stage heading to see its affected Scouts, assign one Activity Bank activity and inspect coverage. Click a requirement for the separate OAS Statement sidebar. Each Scout belongs to only one stage/activity in a week. Different cohorts can run all stages in Week 1, but each Scout must complete every Stage 1 statement before Stage 2, and all Stage 2 statements before Stage 3. This gate applies across streams and themes. Only the assigned stage activity can provide projected opportunities; uncovered statements and other-stage activity coverage do not contribute. Unlinked stage plans are provisional. Projected opportunities unlock later skills only for eligible Scouts in later weeks; stage advancement happens only in a later week once all earlier-stage counts are met. A statement may recur across several weeks until its required opportunities are complete. Assignments assume attendance and successful demonstration. Changes to evidence, scope or week order recalculate names and flag unsuitable locked plans. Actual completion records stay unchanged. The forecaster has been removed.'},
 {id:'activities',title:'Activity Studio',text:'Open Planner → Activity Studio to search or filter activities, create a reusable activity or edit existing coverage. Use the form for the activity name, description, duration, location, themes, equipment, preparation, instructions and OAS coverage. Select an activity in the Activity Bank to read its formatted plan; choose Edit activity to make changes. Suggest coverage uses tags and location; review suggested requirement matches before saving. Linked activities are planning coverage, never evidence of completion.'},
 {id:'monitor',title:'Monitoring requirements coverage',text:'Curriculum Mapping → Requirements Tracker contains the activity and schedule coverage table. Its table separates linked activities, planned versus needed opportunities, weeks scheduled, preferred and alternative locations. The overview beside Scout scope shows total requirements, unaddressed, partially addressed, fully planned and addressed counts. Addressed includes partial and fully planned coverage; these counts do not depend on Scout evidence. Fully planned is a schedule status, not a Scout award. Filter by coverage status, theme, stage, stream or text, or export the complete tracker as CSV.'},
 {id:'backup',title:'Exports, backups and recovery',text:'Use Exporter for a full JSON or YAML backup, or export one dataset. OAS CSV preserves complete records for requirements, progress, activities and schedule; full backups and tree layouts require JSON or YAML. Imports show a preview before applying. Every edit saves a safety checkpoint first; the most recent six are retained. Use Recovery and reset → Review restore to inspect and restore a checkpoint. Keep downloaded backups outside browser storage.'},
 {id:'reset',title:'Resetting data safely',text:'Recovery and reset offers separate actions for achievements, roster plus achievements, schedule, default requirements and factory reset. Choose the smallest action needed, then type RESET to confirm. A full checkpoint is saved before changing data. Restoring default requirements leaves Scout evidence intact. Factory reset clears your local working setup; review the newest checkpoint to undo it.'},
 {id:'mapping',title:'Curriculum Mapping',text:'Curriculum Mapping → Curriculum Editor replaces Skill Trees and Weekly Themes. Select a statement to edit its stage, themes, preferred and alternative locations, mandatory prerequisites and recommended links. Searchable links show statement names and stages; invalid mandatory cycles cannot be saved. Use Duplicate of to group equivalent statements; generation selects at most one group member per night, while repeat sessions in later weeks remain possible. IDs and Scout evidence are preserved, with a recovery checkpoint on save. Existing master prerequisites, private graph layers and tree metadata remain stored; the advanced visual tree editor is still pending in Vue.'},
 {id:'trouble',title:'Troubleshooting and local data',text:'Your Scout records are saved in this browser, not sent to GitHub. If another tab changes local data, reload before editing so you do not overwrite it. If a save fails, stop editing and export a backup or use recovery. The separate legacy comparison app shares the same storage on this origin: close one before editing in the other. A different browser, hostname or port has separate browser storage; transfer a full backup through Exporter and Importer when needed.'}
];
const matches=computed(()=>topics.filter(t=>(t.title+' '+t.text).toLowerCase().includes(query.value.toLowerCase())));
const active=computed(()=>matches.value.find(t=>t.id===selected.value)??matches.value[0]);
const ideas=['Reusable Scout-night templates','Sharing reviewed activities without Scout records','Equipment, venue and leader-resource planning','Calendar dates, holidays, excursions and camps','Installable offline access with explicit recovery guidance','Optional integrations after privacy and permissions design','Accessibility improvements and printable leader guides'];
